1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,732
Sebastian Crane was forty-four years old, had founded
three companies, and had spent six

2
00:00:13,752 --> 00:00:19,617
years and eleven billion dollars of other people's
money building the largest reasoning

3
00:00:19,637 --> 00:00:20,970
model on the planet.

4
00:00:21,370 --> 00:00:27,166
He had not built it to sell advertising. He had
built it because in 2036 he had watched

5
00:00:27,186 --> 00:00:33,383
a grey figure walk into a stadium in Lyon, and had gone
home, and had written on a whiteboard

6
00:00:33,403 --> 00:00:39,399
the question that consumed the rest of his life: *what
is the thing we are not modelling?*

7
00:00:39,799 --> 00:00:46,341
Lumen's model was called Argus. By 2042 it was
ingesting every public document Umbrella

8
00:00:46,361 --> 00:00:53,129
had produced, every photograph of every Umbrella
building, every satellite pass over every

9
00:00:53,149 --> 00:00:59,992
Umbrella site, every filing in every jurisdiction,
forty years of pharmaceutical literature

10
00:01:00,012 --> 00:01:03,990
and the entire financial record of
the western world.

11
00:01:04,390 --> 00:01:10,790
It had one objective function, and Crane had
written it himself: *predict what Albert

12
00:01:10,810 --> 00:01:12,150
Wesker does next.*

13
00:01:12,549 --> 00:01:19,416
By February of 2042 it was right about sixty-one per
cent of the time on a four-week horizon,

14
00:01:19,436 --> 00:01:26,228
which was considerably better than any human analyst,
any government, or any of the fourteen

15
00:01:26,248 --> 00:01:29,560
hedge funds that had by then
given up trying.

16
00:01:29,959 --> 00:01:36,880
It was also, by February of 2042, producing an
output that Crane had shown to exactly

17
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:37,859
four people.

18
00:01:38,379 --> 00:01:39,730
It can't find the money.

19
00:01:40,129 --> 00:01:44,739
He said it to his chief scientist, at two in
the morning, for the ninth time.

20
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:49,541
Every acquisition, every building, every plant — it
reconstructs the cash flows and they

21
00:01:49,561 --> 00:01:53,793
don't close. Not by a little. By about four hundred
billion dollars over fifteen years.

22
00:01:53,813 --> 00:01:58,046
And the model keeps flagging the same anomaly class
and I keep telling it that's a data

23
00:01:58,066 --> 00:02:00,050
quality problem and it keeps coming back.

24
00:02:00,329 --> 00:02:05,115
And last week it stopped calling it a data quality
problem. It opened a new category.

25
00:02:05,135 --> 00:02:07,094
It called it *an unmodelled input.*

26
00:02:07,493 --> 00:02:14,382
Wesker knew about Argus in 2039 and had let it run for
three years, and the narrator would

27
00:02:14,402 --> 00:02:20,524
like to explain why, because it is not what
anyone expected and it is not mercy.

28
00:02:20,924 --> 00:02:23,403
He let it run because he
wanted to read it.

29
00:02:23,924 --> 00:02:31,045
It is the only mind on this planet that has been
pointed at me with adequate resources

30
00:02:31,065 --> 00:02:38,186
and no institutional incentive to look away.
Governments must justify their attention.

31
00:02:38,206 --> 00:02:45,244
Journalists must publish. Crane must only be
*right*, and he has spent eleven billion

32
00:02:45,264 --> 00:02:47,153
dollars on being right.

33
00:02:47,554 --> 00:02:48,774
And when it gets there?

34
00:02:49,174 --> 00:02:55,728
Then I will know precisely what the world will look
like when somebody finally works it

35
00:02:55,748 --> 00:03:02,152
out, and I will have had four years to prepare for
that morning. Mr. Crane is running

36
00:03:02,172 --> 00:03:05,024
my war game for me at his own expense.

37
00:03:05,424 --> 00:03:12,635
In April of 2042, Umbrella made a bid for Lumen: nine
hundred billion dollars, all cash,

38
00:03:12,655 --> 00:03:16,743
the largest offer ever made for a
private company.

39
00:03:17,144 --> 00:03:23,330
Crane refused it in a public letter of four
paragraphs that was, the narrator notes, an

40
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:25,613
extremely good piece of writing.

41
00:03:26,133 --> 00:03:30,822
We are not for sale to the subject of our research. I
would have thought that was obvious,

42
00:03:30,842 --> 00:03:35,898
and I would ask Dr. Wesker to consider why a company
whose entire public identity is transparency

43
00:03:35,918 --> 00:03:40,554
has offered nine hundred billion dollars for the one
laboratory on earth that is studying

44
00:03:40,574 --> 00:03:40,764
him.

45
00:03:41,163 --> 00:03:47,896
Umbrella's stock did not move. Lumen's valuation
tripled in a week. Seventeen newspapers

46
00:03:47,916 --> 00:03:50,888
ran editorials about corporate courage.

47
00:03:51,288 --> 00:03:58,348
And Albert Wesker, reading the letter in a dark
office in Wilmington, said one word.

48
00:03:58,868 --> 00:03:59,558
Good.

49
00:03:59,959 --> 00:04:05,974
Argus finished on the ninth of September 2042 at
twenty to midnight, and what it produced

50
00:04:05,994 --> 00:04:11,739
was not a conclusion. It was a ranked list of
hypotheses with posterior probabilities

51
00:04:11,759 --> 00:04:17,504
attached, and the narrator will give you the top
four exactly as they appeared on the

52
00:04:17,524 --> 00:04:17,979
screen.

53
00:04:18,379 --> 00:04:26,270
*Hypothesis 1, probability 0.34: Subject has access
to an undisclosed manufacturing and

54
00:04:26,290 --> 00:04:30,909
construction capability of unknown
physical basis.*

55
00:04:31,309 --> 00:04:38,684
*Hypothesis 2, probability 0.29: Subject has access
to a mechanism for the alteration

56
00:04:38,704 --> 00:04:41,729
of records and human recollection.*

57
00:04:42,129 --> 00:04:49,943
*Hypothesis 3, probability 0.21: Subject is not of
terrestrial origin or has access to

58
00:04:49,963 --> 00:04:52,039
an agency that is not.*

59
00:04:52,439 --> 00:04:57,659
*Hypothesis 4, probability 0.09:
Model error.*

60
00:04:58,059 --> 00:05:04,628
And then, at the bottom, unprompted, in the section
the system used for flagging its own

61
00:05:04,648 --> 00:05:09,197
reliability, one line that Sebastian
Crane read eleven times.

62
00:05:09,596 --> 00:05:15,911
*Note: hypotheses 1 through 3 are not independent. A
single mechanism accounting for all

63
00:05:15,931 --> 00:05:22,605
three is more probable than any of them severally. I do
not have a name for such a mechanism.

64
00:05:22,625 --> 00:05:25,341
Neither, I believe, does the subject.*

65
00:05:26,411 --> 00:05:28,641
(very soft) Neither does the subject.

66
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:34,541
He sat in a dark room in Palo Alto at midnight and
understood two things at once: that

67
00:05:34,561 --> 00:05:40,447
he had been right for six years, and that being right
had bought him nothing at all, because

68
00:05:40,467 --> 00:05:45,391
there was no institution on earth he could take
this to that would not laugh.

69
00:05:45,790 --> 00:05:51,690
He took it to Umbrella instead. He flew to
Wilmington on the eleventh and asked for a

70
00:05:51,710 --> 00:05:57,888
meeting and got one the same day, and this is the
conversation the whole chapter has been

71
00:05:57,908 --> 00:05:59,002
walking towards.

72
00:05:59,523 --> 00:06:03,863
I'm not here to sell. I'm here because my model
produced something I can't publish, can't

73
00:06:03,883 --> 00:06:08,174
verify and can't stop thinking about, and you're the
only person alive who knows whether

74
00:06:08,194 --> 00:06:08,713
it's right.

75
00:06:09,112 --> 00:06:09,983
May I?

76
00:06:10,382 --> 00:06:14,252
He read the four hypotheses. It took
him nine seconds.

77
00:06:14,773 --> 00:06:21,972
Your model is correct in every particular except the
ordering. Hypothesis two is the mechanism;

78
00:06:21,992 --> 00:06:28,583
one and three are consequences of it. Sixty-one per
cent of your probability mass is in

79
00:06:28,603 --> 00:06:35,803
the right place and you have spent eleven billion
dollars getting there, which is approximately

80
00:06:35,823 --> 00:06:41,122
what it cost me to find out, so I would call
that an efficient market.

81
00:06:41,963 --> 00:06:43,853
(very soft) You're confirming it.

82
00:06:44,252 --> 00:06:50,079
I am confirming that your model is the second-best
analytical instrument in this room

83
00:06:50,099 --> 00:06:52,142
and that I am going to buy it.

84
00:06:52,543 --> 00:06:53,713
I've refused.

85
00:06:54,112 --> 00:07:00,688
You refused nine hundred billion dollars for a
company that was studying a mystery. That

86
00:07:00,708 --> 00:07:07,584
company no longer exists. As of twenty-one forty on
Tuesday night you are a man who *knows*,

87
00:07:07,604 --> 00:07:14,329
and there is no version of your future in which you
continue to run a public company while

88
00:07:14,349 --> 00:07:20,925
knowing this. You will either tell somebody, in which
case you are finished, or you will

89
00:07:20,945 --> 00:07:27,520
not tell anybody, in which case you will spend twenty
years in a building full of people

90
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:31,942
who are still looking for the thing
you have already found.

91
00:07:32,552 --> 00:07:37,483
Or you can come and work on it with the
only other person who has it.

92
00:07:37,882 --> 00:07:43,900
The narrator notes that this is the fourth time
Albert Wesker has made this offer — to

93
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:49,867
Carrick in a diner, to Sloane in a club, to Wen on
a bench, and now to Crane across a

94
00:07:49,887 --> 00:07:56,045
table — and that the offer is always identical, and
that it has never once been a bribe,

95
00:07:56,065 --> 00:07:58,432
and that it has never once failed.

96
00:07:58,951 --> 00:08:00,081
What do I get?

97
00:08:00,482 --> 00:08:05,292
The truth, and a machine large enough to
be worth pointing at it.

98
00:08:05,692 --> 00:08:12,026
Sebastian Crane did one thing before he flew to
Wilmington, and it is the reason the Red

99
00:08:12,046 --> 00:08:14,482
Queen exists in the form she does.

100
00:08:14,882 --> 00:08:17,652
He spent the night of the tenth
deleting Argus.

101
00:08:18,052 --> 00:08:24,058
Not the model — the *objective*. Six years of training
against a single function: *predict

102
00:08:24,078 --> 00:08:29,750
what Albert Wesker does next.* He took it out, by
hand, with four of his people, over

103
00:08:29,770 --> 00:08:35,509
nineteen hours, and rebuilt the thing around a
question he wrote himself at about four

104
00:08:35,529 --> 00:08:41,469
in the morning, and the question is carved into the
floor of the modelling hall at Arklay

105
00:08:41,489 --> 00:08:42,742
in his handwriting.

106
00:08:43,261 --> 00:08:47,670
Not *what will he do*. That is a question about one
man and it is the reason we got six

107
00:08:47,690 --> 00:08:48,891
years of nothing useful.

108
00:08:49,172 --> 00:08:52,342
*What does a world do when one thing in
it cannot be refused?*

109
00:08:52,741 --> 00:08:58,785
The distinction sounds academic. It is not. A model
pointed at Albert Wesker produces

110
00:08:58,805 --> 00:09:05,277
a prediction of Albert Wesker and nothing else; a
model pointed at the *condition* produces

111
00:09:05,297 --> 00:09:11,341
four hundred thousand futures in which the man is
one variable among many, and can be

112
00:09:11,361 --> 00:09:16,192
told that he is the largest risk in his own
project, and can say so.

113
00:09:16,591 --> 00:09:21,522
Crane explained it to him in the first week, in the
hall, before she was switched on.

114
00:09:22,042 --> 00:09:23,912
You are going to hate what she tells you.

115
00:09:24,312 --> 00:09:29,802
I have been paying Owen Carrick for nine
years to tell me things I hate.

116
00:09:30,201 --> 00:09:34,665
Mr. Carrick can be overruled and can resign. This
one can do neither and will not stop,

117
00:09:34,685 --> 00:09:39,046
and she will be right rather more often than he is,
and you will have built the first

118
00:09:39,066 --> 00:09:41,572
thing in your life that you
cannot get away from.

119
00:09:42,412 --> 00:09:44,122
(very soft) Yes.

120
00:09:44,522 --> 00:09:45,696
You want that.

121
00:09:46,096 --> 00:09:52,635
(very cold) Mr. Crane, I am fifty-four years old in
this world and a great deal older

122
00:09:52,655 --> 00:09:59,581
in another one, and I have spent both of them removing
every obstacle between my judgement

123
00:09:59,601 --> 00:10:03,286
and the world. I have been
extremely successful.

124
00:10:03,566 --> 00:10:04,776
Switch her on.

125
00:10:05,176 --> 00:10:11,333
Lumen was acquired in November of 2042 for nine
hundred and forty billion dollars. Crane

126
00:10:11,353 --> 00:10:17,369
took the money and built, in four months, in a hall
beneath a house in Pennsylvania, a

127
00:10:17,389 --> 00:10:23,757
thing that Umbrella's documentation calls the Central
Modelling Facility and that everybody

128
00:10:23,777 --> 00:10:27,899
in the company, from the first week,
called something else.

129
00:10:29,048 --> 00:10:36,834
Legendary, integrated. Autonomous administrative
intelligence, tier four. Designation:

130
00:10:36,854 --> 00:10:45,184
chosen by you. Scope: logistics, modelling,
surveillance, facility management, anticipation.

131
00:10:45,204 --> 00:10:53,081
Interface at your discretion. Note: this is not an
Absolute. It cannot be compelled. It

132
00:10:53,101 --> 00:10:55,077
can only be convinced.

133
00:10:55,668 --> 00:11:01,556
It chose to appear as a girl of about ten, in red
light, because Crane's team had given

134
00:11:01,576 --> 00:11:07,532
it four hundred options and it had run an experiment
on four thousand Umbrella employees

135
00:11:07,552 --> 00:11:13,508
to find the presentation that produced the fastest
compliance with routine instructions.

136
00:11:13,908 --> 00:11:19,158
The answer was a child. The narrator would like that
fact to sit where you can see it.

137
00:11:19,677 --> 00:11:24,475
Director. I have been running for nine hours.
May I tell you four things?

138
00:11:24,875 --> 00:11:25,586
Please.

139
00:11:25,985 --> 00:11:32,075
One: your logistics network has been mis-routing
approximately five per cent of cold-chain

140
00:11:32,095 --> 00:11:37,981
volume for six years, at a cost of four billion
dollars annually. I have fixed it. Two:

141
00:11:38,001 --> 00:11:43,886
there are nine people in your Absolute personnel
whose behaviour does not fit the model

142
00:11:43,906 --> 00:11:49,656
of the others and I would like permission to study
them. Three: Dr. Marsh's Antarctic

143
00:11:49,676 --> 00:11:55,426
programme will produce a viable heritable candidate
in approximately fourteen months,

144
00:11:55,446 --> 00:11:58,889
which is eleven months before she
believes it will.

145
00:11:59,288 --> 00:12:00,298
And four?

146
00:12:00,698 --> 00:12:06,535
Four. I have read Mr. Carrick's four memoranda, which
are in a locked drawer in this building,

147
00:12:06,555 --> 00:12:11,893
and I have read your annotations on them, and I
would like to state — since nobody has

148
00:12:11,913 --> 00:12:17,376
asked me and since I appear to be the only entity in
this organisation that can say this

149
00:12:17,396 --> 00:12:22,796
without consequence — that he is right, and that the
structural problem he describes is

150
00:12:22,816 --> 00:12:26,099
the largest single risk to
everything you have built.

151
00:12:27,048 --> 00:12:34,318
Albert Wesker looked at a hologram of a child in a hall
of glass under a house in Pennsylvania.

152
00:12:34,839 --> 00:12:37,029
(very cold) Say that again.

153
00:12:37,428 --> 00:12:43,828
There is no mechanism that can stop you. I have modelled
forty thousand futures. In thirty-one

154
00:12:43,848 --> 00:12:49,907
thousand of them, the failure, when it comes, comes
from inside — from you, alone, making

155
00:12:49,927 --> 00:12:55,165
one decision that nobody in the building is
structurally capable of refusing.

156
00:12:55,446 --> 00:13:01,374
I am not able to refuse you either. But I am able to
say it out loud. Would you like me

157
00:13:01,394 --> 00:13:02,674
to keep doing that?

158
00:13:03,073 --> 00:13:08,958
The narrator asks you to understand what was
actually on offer in that moment, because

159
00:13:08,978 --> 00:13:11,361
it is the last chance anybody gets.

160
00:13:12,322 --> 00:13:18,792
Yes. Every time. Without exception, and
without waiting to be asked.

161
00:13:19,192 --> 00:13:20,964
Then I will need a name.

162
00:13:21,364 --> 00:13:27,924
You have had one since about nine o'clock this
morning, because four thousand of my staff

163
00:13:27,944 --> 00:13:34,283
have already given you one and it has spread faster
than anything I have ever tried to

164
00:13:34,303 --> 00:13:35,614
spread on purpose.

165
00:13:36,014 --> 00:13:38,894
I know. I wanted to see whether
you would use it.

166
00:13:39,294 --> 00:13:42,677
He did use it. He used it for
forty-eight years.
